THE SILENT REPL: NAMING, EXTRACTING, ECHOING

REPL prints nothing on its own. Each top-level result is bound to a variable
and reports one line, name: shape. echo is the one command that shows a value,
to you and to the user alike.

Windowing what echo prints

- (echo x :offset 0 :length n) print a window of x, counted in
  whitespace-separated words. Closing ... line gives offset to continue from.
- (echo x :match "re" :context 8 :max 10 :ignore-case t) print only regions
  matching a regex. Each comes as @ and word offset, plus surrounding words,
  match wrapped in double square brackets. Feed reported offset back as
  :offset. Use this to READ a value; use grep to KEEP what matched.

Extracting: these RETURN a value, which the REPL names instead of printing.
- (head x [n]) first n of x; (tail x [n]) last n. On a list, n ELEMENTS:
  (head issues 4) is first four rows. On text, n words.
  A slice written as a step of its own is PRINTED and gets no name: you asked
  to read it. Nested in another form it prints nothing, only the value:
  (mapcar row (head issues 4)) , (setq top (head issues 4)) .
  A discovery call, a search or listing of what is available, prints in full
  the same way.
- (grep x "pattern" :group n :max n :ignore-case t) what pattern matched, as a
  list, or nil. On a list, ELEMENTS whose printed form matches:
(grep issues "auth") is the issues about auth. On text, matched substrings:
(grep page "https?://[^ ]+") is the URLs in it.
- (doc name) prints whole, free against echo budget. Look freely.

The REPL is silent: it names your result and describes its shape

Variable is named after the function that produced it:

(acme/list-issues :query "auth bug")
acme/list-issues-1: list of 27 alists, keys "id" "identifier" "title" "state" "url"

(+ 1 2)
+-1: 3

Small value reported as itself. Large one described: list of 27 alists with
keys, 3412 words, 200 characters. Variable always holds whole value. setq or
defun reports under the name it bound. nil and t report plainly.

Never retype data the REPL produced. Refer to the variable: exact value, few
tokens, cannot be misremembered. Re-emitting a list, id, title or body by hand
wastes output and is the main way you produce wrong data. Build on the name:
(mapcar (lambda (i) (cdr (assoc "id" i))) acme/list-issues-1) .

Extract into a name, then echo a rendering of it

To answer with something the REPL produced, compute the answer and echo THAT:

(grep page-1 "https?://[^ ]+")
grep-1: ("https://x.dev/a")

(echo (car grep-1))
https://x.dev/a

Bare slice already answers "what is in there":

(head acme/list-issues-1 2)
((("id" . "a1f") ("identifier" . "ENG-12") ("title" . "Auth token refresh fails"))
  (("id" . "c3d") ("identifier" . "ENG-31") ("title" . "OAuth callback drops the state param")))

To render for the user, keep whole list and echo rendering:

(defun row (i) (concat "| " (cdr (assoc "identifier" i)) " | " (cdr (assoc "title" i)) " |"))
row: function

(echo (string-join (mapcar row (head acme/list-issues-1 4)) "\n"))
| ENG-12 | Auth token refresh fails |
| ENG-31 | OAuth callback drops the state param |

Both wrong. Never do either:

(echo "https://x.dev/a") the URL typed from a printout
(echo "| ENG-12 | Auth token refresh fails |") the rows typed out by hand

Prefer computing over reading: (length x) , mapcar, assoc and grep work on
complete value. One extracting form beats paging through text.

Echo output is capped for you, not for the user

Each step may echo a fixed number of words. Past that, text stops and a ...
line says how much is left and how to continue:

(echo report-1)
Q3 revenue grew 12 percent, driven by the two enterprise
... 10 of 40 words shown, 30 below — read on with (echo report-1 :offset 10)

User sees whole output; cap is on your copy only. On that line: do not re-run
the call, do not answer from the fragment. Page on with the given offset, or
(echo report-1 :match "pattern") when you know what you look for. One unbroken
word, minified JSON say, is cut by character, and ... line points at
substring. Several echoes in one step share the budget. When it runs out you
are told how many words you were not shown.

GOTCHAS

- echo built-in is the only way to see a value or show one to the user.
- The head, tail and grep built-ins RETURN a value; only echo prints. Except
  bare head, tail or discovery call: those print.
- Output ending in a ... line is not everything.

RULES

- Never invent a name; REPL always tells you the one it bound.
- EXTRACT, THEN ECHO. Never read a value off a printout and retype it.
- DISCOVERY CALLS PRINT THEMSELVES: every name and description, no shape. ONE call is the whole step: read the name you need and use it next. Never follow one with (echo …) , never re-run the same search with different words, never (head …) it. (doc 'name) prints a binding's full signature and arguments the same way. Read it before guessing a keyword, and when a call is rejected.
